      "chapters": [
        {
          "id": "opening",
          "label": "Same rows, one definition",
          "caption": "Four queries keep returning the same rows, but now get their visibility rule from one function.",
          "role": "opening",
          "body": "Lists and searches hide child Conversations and unkept Quick questions (private side threads a reader opens from a message). Four places wrote both conditions by hand. Now they call `listedConversation()`; access, scope, and sort filters stay with each caller. No behavior change is intended. The payoff is next: Walkthroughs add a Conversation lists must hide, and that becomes one edit. Review risk is small: check each caller lost exactly the two conditions, and that the root-only queries left out should stay.",
          "view": {
            "pr": 3071,
            "show": [
              "listed",
              "feed-rows",
              "feed-token",
              "board",
              "brief-search",
              "agent-search",
              "e-feed-rows",
              "e-feed-token",
              "e-board",
              "e-brief",
              "e-agent"
            ],
            "highlight": ["listed"],
            "added": ["listed"],
            "broken": []
          },
          "evidence": [],
          "decisions": [],
          "asks": [
            "Why not change stats and Locations in the same PR?",
            "Which PRs must land before this one?"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "shared-filter",
          "label": "The listed filter",
          "caption": "`listedConversation()` returns the root check and the unkept Quick question check as one SQL condition.",
          "role": "other",
          "body": "The new function joins the two `isNull` checks with `and` [1]. Its doc comment sets the boundary: it is for what lists and searches may show, not for structural root checks such as retention. A later change can replace the body with a `kind` check without touching callers. The non-null assertion on `and(...)` is safe because both arguments are always defined.",
          "evidence": [
            {
              "kind": "code",
              "pr": 3071,
              "path": "packages/junior/src/chat/conversations/sql/listed.ts",
              "side": "head",
              "start": 1,
              "end": 15
            }
          ],
          "decisions": [
            {
              "id": "hide-new-kinds-here",
              "claim": "Hiding a new Conversation kind from lists only needs an edit to `listedConversation()`.",
              "concern": "Root-only queries such as stats, Locations, and the Conversation page would still count or open that kind unless changed too.",
              "origin": { "kind": "assumed" }
            }
          ],
          "asks": ["Should purge use this too?"]
        },
        {
          "id": "feed-token",
          "label": "Feed rows match the token",
          "caption": "The feed rows and the feed change token now share one filter, so they cannot drift apart.",
          "role": "other",
          "body": "The feed change token (`conversationFeedVersionQuery`) tells the client when to refetch the feed. It must cover the same rows the feed returns; if it counted a row the feed hides, the token would change while the feed did not. Before, both queries copied the two conditions [1][2]. Now both call `listedConversation()` [3][4], so they match by construction.",
